# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cluster-api

# ⚠ THREE UPSTREAM PROJECTS, EACH SERVING ONE OF THE THREE OBJECTS THIS CHART RENDERS, AND THEY ARE
# VERSIONED INDEPENDENTLY. Read against the repositories' own CRD YAML and Go types on 2026-08-12,
# not against a README and not against a quickstart template.
upstream-api: cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta2, controlplane.cluster.x-k8s.io/v1alpha2, infrastructure.cluster.x-k8s.io/v1alpha1

cluster-api: https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cluster-api             # v1.14.0, released 2026-08-11
control-plane-provider: https://github.com/clastix/cluster-api-control-plane-provider-kamaji  # v0.20.0
infrastructure-provider: https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cluster-api-provider-kubevirt     # v0.11.2
datastore-engine: https://github.com/clastix/kamaji

# ⚠ A CONSTRAINT ON THE MANAGEMENT CLUSTER, NOT ON THIS CHART, AND IT HAD NOWHERE TO LIVE UNTIL NOW.
# This chart's `Cluster` names its control plane and its infrastructure by {apiGroup, kind, name} and
# Cluster API resolves the version from a `cluster.x-k8s.io/<contract>` label on the PROVIDER's CRD.
# cluster-api-provider-kubevirt v0.11.2 is still labelled `cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta1: v1alpha1`, so it
# resolves only through CAPI's GetCompatibleVersions shim, which that function's own comment schedules
# for removal in CAPI v1.16. Installing Cluster API v1.16 or later before the KubeVirt provider
# relabels produces a Cluster whose infrastructureRef cannot be resolved at all.
#
# Re-checked 2026-08-18: CAPK v0.11.2 (2026-03-03) is still its newest release and still carries the
# v1beta1 label; CAPI v1.14.0 is still the newest, so v1.16 has not shipped.
# conformance.yaml § owed, `capk-crd-carries-the-v1beta1-contract-label`.
max-cluster-api: "<v1.16"
max-cluster-api-reviewed: 2026-08-18

reviewed: 2026-08-12

# ── What the review established, and which claims are weak ────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE Cluster IS RENDERED AT v1beta2 AND UPSTREAM'S OWN KAMAJI+KUBEVIRT TEMPLATE STILL EMITS
# v1beta1. `config/crd/bases/cluster.x-k8s.io_clusters.yaml` marks v1beta1 `deprecated: true`,
# `storage: false`; v1.14.0's release notes say it "is on track to be unserved in CAPI v1.16". The
# control-plane provider's `templates/kubevirt/capi-kamaji-kubevirt-class.yaml` nonetheless emits
# `cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta1` and `spec.topology.class`, while its own controller imports the v1beta2
# Go package. Copying the template would have pinned this platform to a version that stops being
# served; the template is a starting point, which is what ADR-010 already says.
#
# ⚠ v1beta2 REPLACED corev1.ObjectReference WITH ContractVersionedObjectReference — {apiGroup, kind,
# name}, all required, and NO apiVersion. `api/core/v1beta2/common_types.go`: "a reference to a
# resource for which the version is inferred from contract labels." So the version this platform's
# refs resolve to is decided by a LABEL ON SOMEBODY ELSE'S CRD, and that is the single most fragile
# upstream fact this row rests on — see conformance.yaml § owed,
# `capk-crd-carries-the-v1beta1-contract-label`.
#
# ⚠ KamajiControlPlane IS v1alpha2 AND THERE IS NO v1alpha1 TO FALL BACK TO. v0.20.0's CRD serves
# exactly one version. Its `config/metadata.yaml` is also the first to claim the v1beta2 Cluster API
# contract (minor 20 -> v1beta2; 19 and below -> v1beta1), which is what makes the Cluster's own
# version choice consistent rather than merely newer.
#
# ⚠ FIVE `+kubebuilder:default` MARKERS ON KamajiControlPlaneSpec, AND THIS IS WHY `Matches` IS
# CONTAINMENT. `replicas=2`; `registry="registry.k8s.io"`; a whole `kubelet` object
# ({preferredAddressTypes: {InternalIP, ExternalIP, Hostname}, cgroupfs: systemd}); a whole `network`
# object ({serviceType: LoadBalancer}); and `network.serviceType="LoadBalancer"` again on the nested
# field. A real API server writes four keys back that this platform never sent. Cluster API's own
# ClusterSpec, by contrast, carries NO defaults and its mutating webhook is a no-op for a
# non-topology Cluster — which is worth recording precisely because it is the opposite of the
# assumption a reader would carry over.
#
# ⚠ THE ONE THAT IS A SECURITY DECISION: network.serviceType DEFAULTS TO LoadBalancer. Rendering
# nothing would publish every tenant's Kubernetes API server on a public address. `ClusterIP` is
# rendered explicitly, and the reason there is no allow-list instead is below.
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NOWHERE UPSTREAM TO PUT A CIDR ALLOW-LIST, WHICH IS WHY THIS TYPE HAS NO EXPOSURE
# PROPERTY. The KubeVirt provider's `ServiceSpecTemplate` (api/v1alpha1/kubevirtcluster_types.go) has
# exactly ONE field — `type` — with no ports, no selector and no loadBalancerSourceRanges; Kamaji's
# `NetworkComponent` offers serviceAnnotations and serviceLabels and no source ranges either.
# docs/plan/12 § Cross-cutting decisions requires an explicit list on any exposure. Second sighting of
# an upstream ServiceSpec with nowhere to put a firewall — charts/managed/seaweedfs found the first.
#
# ⚠ THE KubevirtCluster CARRIES `cluster.x-k8s.io/managed-by: kamaji` AND WITHOUT IT TWO CONTROLLERS
# OWN THE SAME FIELD. Read in `controllers/kubevirtcluster_controller.go`: the KubeVirt provider
# returns immediately for an externally-managed cluster, creating no load-balancer Service, no SSH-key
# Secret, and never setting status.ready. The Kamaji provider then patches spec.controlPlaneEndpoint
# and status.ready onto it — through a HARDCODED SWITCH over infrastructure kinds
# (`kamajicontrolplane_controller_cluster_patch.go`) in which KubevirtCluster is a listed case with
# patchStatus=true.
#
# ⚠ THE DataStore IS CLUSTER-SCOPED. `charts/kamaji/crds/kamaji.clastix.io_datastores.yaml` is
# `scope: Cluster`. That is why this platform NAMES one rather than creating one per tenant, and why
# ADR-009's "a dedicated etcd per tenant" is not honoured — conformance.yaml § owed,
# `datastore-is-shared-and-adr-009-says-it-should-not-be`. `dataStoreName` has no default marker and
# no required marker, and Kamaji's validating webhook (`internal/webhook/handlers/tcp_datastore.go`)
# nonetheless Gets the named DataStore and refuses with "<name> DataStore does not exist" — so the
# field is optional in the schema and mandatory in fact. `default` is the name Kamaji's own chart
# creates (`defaultDatastoreName: default`, with a kamaji-etcd subchart).
#
# ⚠ clusterNetwork.serviceDomain IS NOT DEFAULTED BY CLUSTER API AND IS IMMUTABLE ONCE KAMAJI HAS IT.
# No +kubebuilder:default, and the mutating webhook never touches it (grepped for ServiceDomain and
# cluster.local in both the v1.9.7 and v1.14.0 webhook files: zero hits). The Kamaji provider copies
# it into the tenant control plane's clusterDomain, which carries `self == oldSelf` with the message
# "changing the cluster domain is not supported". So it is written at creation, because it cannot be
# added later.
#
# ⚠ ONLY THE FIRST CIDR BLOCK IS READ. Cluster API's NetworkRanges.CIDRBlocks allows up to a hundred;
# the Kamaji provider reads CIDRBlocks[0] for pods and for services. A dual-stack request would
# silently become single-stack, which is why this chart's surface takes one string per family rather
# than a list.
#
# ⚠ VERSION SKEW IS ENFORCED UPSTREAM IN TWO PLACES AND BY THIS PLATFORM IN NEITHER. Kamaji's
# `tcp_version.go` refuses a version above its own bundled kubeadm, refuses downgrades, and refuses a
# two-minor jump ("a minor version in a non-sequential mode"); Cluster API's topology webhook does the
# same for topology clusters. The kubelet rule — three minors, since Kubernetes 1.28 — is the
# Kubernetes version-skew policy's. conformance.yaml § owed, `version-skew-is-not-enforced`.
#
# ── The weak claims, named because a field list is weaker evidence than a struct ───────────────
#
# ⚠ `spec.apiServer.resources`, `spec.controllerManager.resources` and `spec.scheduler.resources` ARE
# RENDERED ON EVIDENCE OF A FIELD NAME AND NOT OF A GO TYPE. KamajiControlPlaneFields declares
# ApiServer, ControllerManager and Scheduler as `ControlPlaneComponent`, and that struct's members
# were NOT read. If it does not carry a `resources` block in the corev1.ResourceRequirements shape,
# every KamajiControlPlane this chart renders is refused by the CRD's own schema at apply time —
# visibly, per object, with the API server's own message, which is the failure mode this platform
# prefers to a silent one. It is called out here for the same reason charts/managed/seaweedfs-bucket's
# SOURCE calls out `clusterRef`.
#
# ── The version pins, read on 2026-08-18 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE BINDING PAGE IS NOT THE KUBERNETES RELEASE PAGE. `ManagedClusters.PinnedPatch` is rendered
# both as a control-plane version here and as the node-image TAG by charts/managed/kubernetes-agentpool,
# and quay.io/capk/ubuntu-2404-container-disk publishes one tag per MINOR: v1.31.5, v1.32.1, v1.33.5,
# v1.34.1. So the pin is chosen from that list. 1.32 -> v1.32.1, 1.33 -> v1.33.5.
#
# The previous pins, v1.32.9 and v1.33.4, were real Kubernetes releases (1.32 ended at 1.32.13; 1.33
# is at 1.33.13) and were tags of nothing, so every worker VM pulled an image that does not exist.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THE CHOICE COSTS, AND IT IS ON THE 1.32 ROW: v1.32.1 was built in January 2025 and carries
# every fix from 1.32.2 to 1.32.13. A newer control plane with no bootable node image is a cluster
# with no nodes, which is worse and quieter.
#
# ⚠ AND BOTH OFFERED MINORS ARE OUT OF UPSTREAM SUPPORT — 1.32 since 2026-02-28, 1.33 since
# 2026-06-28; the supported minors that day were 1.34, 1.35 and 1.36. That is not a pin move: it is a
# new api-version, because ManagedClusters.Versions is an AllowedValues a stored body carries.
# conformance.yaml § owed, `offered-minors-are-out-of-support`.
#
# ⚠ STILL UNCHECKED, BECAUSE IT NEEDS A RUNNING KAMAJI RATHER THAN A REGISTRY: whether a pin exceeds
# the install's bundled kubeadm, which `tcp_version.go` refuses at admission.
pinned-patches-reviewed: 2026-08-18
upstream-webhook: kamaji core validates the datastore, the version and the certificate SANs on the TenantControlPlane the control-plane provider creates; the control-plane provider itself ships no webhook (config/default/kustomization.yaml has ../webhook commented out); cluster-api ships both a validating and a mutating webhook for Cluster, and the mutating one is a no-op for a non-topology cluster
